  "query_preview": "..A shortwave ridge will continue to cross Utah today,\nwith the ridge axis shifting east of Utah tonight. This will put\nUtah under an increasing southwesterly flow aloft through \ntomorrow ahead of the next storm system. The progressive nature of\nthe pattern will keep strong inversions at bay, and temperatures\ntomorrow are expected to climb to 5-10 degrees above \nclimatological normals across northern Utah while southern Utah \nwill trend closer to normal. \n\nThe next storm system is expected to start bringing increasing \nshowers to the area tomorrow night, though the bulk of the\nmoisture will likely hold off until the Tuesday morning through\nTuesday night timeframe. Precipitation will initially occur under\na southwesterly flow aloft and will be more favorable for the \nsouthern Utah mountains. However, snow levels will initially \nremain on the high side, generally above 8500 feet through at\nleast Tuesday morning. A cold front then looks to cross the area \nTuesday afternoon into Tuesday evening, switching the flow to a \nmore westerly direction behind it. Precipitation will then become\nmore favorable for northern and central Utah mountains while snow\nlevels fall through Tuesday night. H7 temperatures are currently\nforecast to bottom out at around -8C towards the tail end of the \nprecipitation event. This could drop snow levels as low as 5000 \nft, which would bring snow to the higher valleys and potentially \nthe Wasatch Front benches.\n\nPrecipitation amounts for this storm should not be too dissimilar\nto the last one, although QPF is expected to be a bit higher for\nthis one. Overall, expect snow amounts in the mountains to be in\nthe 4-9 inch range above 8000 feet, with higher amounts possible\nin the upper Cottonwoods, Tushars, and near Brian Head. Most\nhigher valleys and bench areas are unlikely to see more than 1\ninch. A Winter Weather Advisory has been issued for the southern\nUtah mountains, but will allow later shifts to evaluate the need\nfor an Advisory for other zones, especially the Wasatch Mountains\nwhich currently look borderline.\n\nA brief break in the weather is expected by Wednesday afternoon\nthrough Wendesday night before yet another storm system starts to\nimpact the area. While there is high confidence in a late week\nstorm, there is still quite a bit of model spread regarding its\ntiming and evolution. However, there is potential for this to be a\nlonger-duration storm (Thursday morning through Friday) and also\nperhaps a bit colder. As such, there is currently around a 45%\nchance for some Wasatch Front valley floors to see measurable \nsnow, and a 10-20% chance of seeing at least 1 inch before high\npressure builds back into the area on Saturday.",
